<tr id="S6.T1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">C5</th>
<th id="S6.T1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="S6.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mathrm{e}{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mathrm{e}{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S6.T1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.0</td>
<td id="S6.T1.3.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">✓</td>
<td id="S6.T1.3.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">61.46</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first perform a coarse search to identify a promising region for <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the warm-up mode. As shown in <a href="#S6.T1" title="In 6.2.1 Hyperparameter Adjustment ‣ 6.2 Results Analysis ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Vision Transformers that Never Stop Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (left), the results indicate that <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values in the range <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently yield better performance. Based on this analysis, we fix the warm-up strategy to RMS-like updates and window size <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=20</annotation></semantics></math> for subsequent experiments. The final hyperparameter configuration of ARROW is summarized in <a href="#S6.T1" title="In 6.2.1 Hyperparameter Adjustment ‣ 6.2 Results Analysis ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Vision Transformers that Never Stop Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (right). We additionally evaluate exponential decay schedules for <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> with rates <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.999</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.98</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.97" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.97</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.97</annotation></semantics></math>, considering both global and per-task decay schemes (Appendix). However, neither strategy improves over the best fixed-parameter configuration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.2 </span>Ablation Study</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the contribution of individual components in ARROW, we disable key terms and vary the application scope across blocks. Specifically, we test settings with <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply ARROW to different subsets of attention blocks (all blocks, the last six blocks, and the last block). When <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>, ARROW degenerates into a first-order optimizer, removing both directional damping and curvature-based reshaping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S6.T2" title="In 6.2.2 Ablation Study ‣ 6.2 Results Analysis ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Vision Transformers that Never Stop Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, applying ARROW to the last attention blocks yields the best performance. This confirms the necessity of its key components and supports the findings in <a href="#S4" title="4 The Loss of Plasticity in ViTs ‣ Vision Transformers that Never Stop Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that deep attention modules are the contributors to plasticity degradation and the effective targets for geometry-aware optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Ablation study results of ARROW with controlling terms of <math id="S6.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and effected blocks.</figcaption>
